<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Lindau</h1><p class="st-meta">1275 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Lindau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Island city in Lake Constance, a trading gateway to Italy and Switzerland and one of four cities that signed the Tetrapolitan Confession in 1530.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lindau grew around a convent of noble canonesses founded on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. The town became an important market and port, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> era it gained recognition as an imperial city, around 1275, gradually limiting the convent's rights.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of patricians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> governed. Lindau thrived on trade over the lake and through the Alpine passes to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Milan</a>, served by the Lindau messenger service. The city hosted an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diet</a> in 1496. It accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and in 1530 joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-memmingen">Memmingen</a> in presenting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrapolitan-confession">Tetrapolitan Confession</a>, later adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 Lindau went to Prince Bretzenheim, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1804 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1805. It remains Bavarian, Bavaria's only town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, linked to the mainland by bridge and railway causeway.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1275 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.07%;width:10.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1275 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lindau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lindau</dd></dl></aside></div>
